## Internet observatory, the survey of a domain: where its names and
## addresses are, what answers for it, the routers on the way, which
## networks carry its route, and where the internet has an outage right
## now. site.nexo serves it as a website.
##     nexo test survey.nexo    the parts that read the answers
## Every figure is asked when a survey runs: DNS to two resolvers over
## HTTPS, places, networks and routes to RIPEstat, the routers on the way
## to RIPE Atlas (the traceroutes its probes already run to the domain),
## exchange points to PeeringDB, certificates to crt.sh, outages to IODA,
## the cables to the Submarine Cable Map, and what is open at the site's
## address to Shodan InternetDB (the observatory scans nothing itself). Each run is kept in
## observatory.db, so the page says what changed since the last one.
## A survey ends with its alerts: what an operator should look at (a
## zone not signed, a subdomain left hanging, mail anyone can forge, a
## route with no ROA, a second origin or a more specific prefix from
## another network, a single upstream, a port that should be closed, a
## certificate about to end), each with what to do, the most serious
## first, and a mark from 0 to 100 for DNS, mail, routes and the site.
## What nobody publishes is not shown: there is no public feed of cable
## faults, so a cable is only marked when it lands in a country with an
## outage signal, or named as a candidate where a trace crosses a sea.
## With RIPE_ATLAS_KEY set to "Key <your key>", a domain nobody traces
## gets a measurement started; its routers are read on a later run.
## A module has no policy of its own: the program that uses it lists what
## it reaches.
use nexo.db
use python subprocess as subprocess
use python platform as platform
use python shutil as shutil

db = connect("observatory.db")

## One run of the observatory, kept to compare the next one with.
@table
type Run:
    id: id
    domain: str
    moment: time
    address: str
    route: str
    seeing: int
    total: int
    arrived: int
    probes: int
    status: int
    ms: int
    front: str
    rpki: str
    origin: str

## A text kept for later: the cables, and the last survey of each domain.
@table
type Kept:
    id: id
    name: str
    moment: time
    body: str

## A traceroute this observatory asked RIPE Atlas to run.
@table
type Started:
    id: id
    domain: str
    measurement: int
    moment: time

RESOLVER = "https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query"
RIPE = "https://stat.ripe.net/data"
IODA = "https://api.ioda.inetintel.cc.gatech.edu/v2/outages/alerts"
CABLES = "https://www.submarinecablemap.com/api/v3"
ATLAS = "https://atlas.ripe.net/api/v2"
GOOGLE = "https://dns.google/resolve"
PEERINGDB = "https://www.peeringdb.com/api"
CRT = "https://crt.sh"
SHODAN = "https://internetdb.shodan.io"
SPF_SIGN = regex("^[+~?-]")
LETTERS = regex("[^a-z]+")
HOP_LINE = regex("^\\s*(\\d+)\\s+(.*)$")
ADDRESS = regex("\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+")
HOP_TIME = regex("<?([0-9.]+)\\s*ms")
LAST_DOT = regex("\\.$")
DOMAIN = regex("^([a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]*[a-z0-9])?\\.)+[a-z]{{2,}$")
ORGANISATION = regex("O=\"?([^,\"]+)")

## Where to take a probe from when a traceroute has thousands of them.
PROBE_COUNTRIES = ["US", "BR", "DE", "ZA", "IN", "JP", "AU", "GB"]

## The cities of the RIS route collectors: latitude and longitude.
CITIES = {
    "Amsterdam, NL": [52.37, 4.90],
